description:
pkix-path/src/lib.rs:910-914

`prune_policy_tree` allocates a `Vec<ObjectIdentifier>` on every iteration of the pruning loop:

```rust
let child_policies: Vec<der::asn1::ObjectIdentifier> = tree
    .iter()
    .filter(|n| n.depth == d)
    .map(|n| n.valid_policy)
    .collect();
```

This Vec is only used in the `retain` closure that follows. Since `ObjectIdentifier` is `Copy`, the collect into a Vec could be replaced with a closure that re-iterates the tree inline — avoiding the allocation entirely:

```rust
tree.retain(|n| {
    if n.depth != prune_depth {
        return true;
    }
    // Re-scan children inline — tree is not mutated here.
    tree.iter().any(|child| {
        child.depth == d && n.expected_policy_set.contains(&child.valid_policy)
    })
});
```

However, note that `retain` takes `&mut self`, so borrowing `tree` inside the closure is a borrow conflict. The `collect()` pattern works around this. An alternative that avoids the allocation is to snapshot indices or use a bitset.

A P3 note: the correct fix is to either accept the current collect() (it's correct and the policy tree is small), or restructure the loop to avoid the dual borrow. Document the reason for the collect() if kept.
